the democratic republic of congo has  seen a surge of violence in recent weeks  attacks by militants and inter-community  conflict has forced millions of people  to flee their homes  the u.n says that ongoing fighting is  making it extremely difficult to reach  many people  in areas now our reporter mario muller  traveled to ituri  one of the two provinces in the  northeast of the country  officially under a state of siege she  met many people  taking refuge there  hardly any other region has suffered  atrocities by militias as badly as  congo's ituri province  we are on the road with a norwegian aid  organization over a distance of just 60  kilometers we pass through about 20  checkpoints  controlled by various rebel groups as  well as the congolese army  a militia checkpoint it's mostly boys  carrying guns  their weapons are concealed but the  threat is real they ask for money but a  few words persuade them to let us pass  today  we reached the camp for internally  displaced people in draw drawdro  it's one of many in the drc about five  and a half million of the country's  estimated 105 million people are  displaced  more than anywhere else in africa  barely managed to escape her village  when a militia attacked it  she was on the run with her children  when she realized her daughter was  missing  i decided to go back to find and save  her i was convinced she was still alive  but unfortunately she was already dead i  was so sad just devastated  when i walked past them they shot at me  i had no idea if i'd survive  i gradually lost all my strength i was  having difficulty breathing  then i slipped into some bush i lost all  hope i was sure i was going to die  she survived but found her four-year-old  daughter murdered  as soon as she was released from  hospital she gathered her five other  children and fled to this camp  food water and medical care are in short  supply  but at least they are safe here she says  and that was our correspondent mario  muller reporting from itauri  you can find more of her coverage on the  website  dw.com in the meantime now i am joined  by jackie keegan she  is the head of the unhcr's sub-office in  goma and monitors the situation in north  kivu and in natori  thank you so much for joining us here on  dw news um you know as we've been  hearing in in that reporting the  democratic republic of congo has the  highest number of displaced people  in africa one recent estimate said  that more than 120 armed groups are  fighting in its four eastern provinces  what are what are driving these  conflicts  there are actually a number of conflicts  in eastern drc which have different  causes  but which are fed by a number of the  same problems so  the area suffers from a combination of  weak land and mineral resource  regulation the presence of many  thousands of light weapons and cheap  ammunition  weak police and justice sectors the  isolation caused by poor roads and  fragile communications the special envoy  from the great lakes has spoken about  the impact of regional interest in the  drc and the presence of foreign armed  actors  ultimately without a global commitment  to establishing the rule of law  to reducing impunity and to building an  economy that serves local residents  conflict will remain a problem in the  east for  many years to come it's it's challenging  as we have seen uh do you know to reach  those who who are  in need amid amid these conflicts tell  us you know how do you do it at the u.n  how do you reach these people  many displaced people most find support  from their neighbors people like  themselves who  take them in when they have to run from  violence the  unhcr and others work working more and  more closely with local authorities and  communities  in advance of flights during moments of  calm to strengthen their capacity to  respond to needs as they arise they're  the first responders and we need to help  them  the idea is to help communities to  design and implement programming that  responds best to local needs  and prepares them to to better  accommodate people so that might mean  uh building additional shelter building  building wells that people might be able  to use so that they can avoid going too  far  uh to to put themselves at risk of rape  for example just in the search of water  but when there is massive displacement  the international community does need to  help directly  and so we negotiate access with whomever  is is in control and we we plan very  carefully so that we're able to get in  and and distribute what we need to  distribute ultimately too much  falls on the shoulders of of locals  however  what needs to be done to break the cycle  of violence from from what you  have seen there locally  security remains the responsibility of  the state and  ultimately it will be important for  civilian authorities including police  and justice systems to function for the  good of everybody  in a predictable way establishing and  maintaining regulation of land  management mining management  agricultural sectors the the um the  raising of  of cattle and the sale of cattle that is  the support and the distribution of  national id cards  can have a massive impact on on daily  life on on direct protection and and  security risks  and can at the same time reduce conflict  drivers while  giving perhaps a bit of space for a  development perspective and  and maybe economic opportunities that go  beyond  the the immediate a new government is  arriving today in north kivu and natori  the president indicated that it was his  priority to address the violence and and  so declared this this state of siege in  north kiba is mandatory as you said and  it's hoped that the military will be  part of the  uh will be part of the solution and and  that indeed part of their plan will be  to  be accompanied by and to support the  establishment of strong  civilian institutions in areas where  military operations are undertaken  so that people are able to rebuild trust  in local and national services and in  in the possibility that their civilian  leaders will be able to support  their safety and stability this is going  to take a while  uh but but ultimately we we need all  different parts  of of the the congolese government  and and its supporters to to work  together jackie keegan of the unhcr and  goma in the drc thank you so much for  joining us  thank you
 

